WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.500
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.920
In this series, we talk quite a bit about
neural network-based learning methods that

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:14.420
are able to generate new images for us from
some sort of sparse description, like a written

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.250
sentence, or a set of controllable parameters.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:23.640
These can enable us mere mortals without artistic
skills to come up with novel images.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:28.740
However, one thing that comes up with almost
every single one of these techniques is the

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.020
lack of artistic control.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.770
You see, if we provide a very coarse input,
there are many-many different ways for the

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.920
neural networks to create photorealistic images
from them.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.249
So, how do we get more control over these
results?

00:00:42.249 --> 00:00:48.589
An earlier paper from NVIDIA generated human
faces for us and used a latent space technique

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:52.329
that allows us some more fine-grained control
over the images.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:54.850
It is beyond amazing.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:01:00.239
But, these are called latent variables because
they represent the inner thinking process

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:05.610
of the neural network, and they do not exactly
map to our intuition of facial features in

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:06.610
reality.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:11.410
And now, have a look at this new technique
that allows us to edit the geometry of the

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:17.940
jawline of a person, put a smile on someone’s
face in a more peaceful way than seen in some

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:25.110
Batman movies, or, remove the sunglasses and
add some crazy hair at the same time.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:30.310
Even changing the hair of someone while adding
an earring with a prescribed shape is also

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.310
possible.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.310
Whoa!

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:37.780
And I just keep talking and talking about
artistic control, so it’s great that these

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.890
shapes are supported, but what about an other
important aspect of artistic control, for

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.760
instance, colors?

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.270
Yup, that is also supported.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.560
Here you see that the color of the woman’s
eyes can be changed, and, the technique also

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.020
understands the concept of makeup as well.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.020
How cool is that!

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.860
Not only that, but it is also blazing fast.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:06.460
It takes roughly 50 milliseconds to create
these images with the resolution of 512 by

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:11.030
512, so in short, we can do this about 20
times per second.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:15.260
Make sure to have a look at the paper that
also contains a validation section against

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:21.530
other techniques and reference results, turns
out there is such a thing as a reference result

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:27.170
in this case, which is really cool, and you
will also find a novel style loss formulation

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.190
that makes all this crazy wizardry happen.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.969
No web app for this one, however, the source
code is also available free of charge and

00:02:34.969 --> 00:02:39.180
under a permissive license, so let the experiments
begin!

